 First `cargo test --lib` and a build: everything merged on 2026-10-16 (Tectonic, the Nether, the title screen, the light thread, lazy noise, distant terrain) was written without a Rust toolchain; only the std-only crates (`tectonic`, `embeddium`, `lod`) and the `world/gen` tests were run, in a Linux scratch crate. Then, in the easy-first order: the Nether's ghast, fireball and pig-zombie (M11), the rest of M14 (options, chat, models), the map item (M20); M21 (redstone) goes on from part 5 (the piston shaft model).
 
 ## Changelog
+- 2026-10-16 (2): Pregen button + persistence (Voxy World Gen's pre-generation; the first LoD patch compiled and ran, the screenshots still showed guessed trees floating in the air and the user asked for the Voxy feature itself). Written WITHOUT a toolchain again: not compiled, not run; `cargo test --lib --release lod` / `touch_ui` first (`a_tile_file_round_trips`, `pregen_goes_nearest_first_and_counts_chunks` new; `a_cell_keeps_...` changed).
+    - Pause menu, top-left: `Pregen: off, N chunks` / `Pregen: N chunks (tap to stop)` (`LayoutRects::pregen`, `PointerRole::Pregen`, `TouchUi::take_pregen`, `Lod::toggle_pregen` / `Lod::pregen`). On: the worker (`pregen_step` = `DistanceGraph.findWork`) makes the tile nearest the player that is not real yet within `RADIUS` (a tile = Voxy's 4 x 4 chunk batch) with its trees, 16 chunks a tile, one per loop turn; a waiting view build or ingest goes first. Stops by itself when the range is done. The tiles it makes are meshed again (`made` channel -> `dirty`) when they were drawn as a guess.
+    - Persistence (`ChunkPersistence`): tiles with trees are written to `<world>/lod/<x>_<z>.lod` (`Store::flush`, after every job; temp file + rename; runs of equal columns, magic `L2` + world type + trees; another type or a cut file is ignored) and read back by `Store::tile` before generating, so pre-generation survives a restart and ingested edits are kept. A world reset wipes the folder with the rest.
+    - Floating trees: a coarse cell's leaf mass now stands on the ground (`resolve`), only step 1 has a trunk and a gap under the crown.
+    - ponytail: the count is tiles in memory or on disk x 16, not distinct chunks of the ring; no pause between tiles (the worker thread is busy while it is on: expect a warm phone); the Nether has no LoD, the button says so; tiles beyond ~13 tiles of the player are not made. UNVERIFIED: speed per tile on a device, disk size (a rough 10-60 KB a tile).
 - 2026-10-16: LoD rework: real chunks become the LoD (Voxy's ingest, `voxy_worldgen_v2` as the reference). Written WITHOUT a Rust toolchain: not compiled, not run; run `cargo test --lib --release lod` first (`a_tree_is_leaves_over_its_own_ground`, `a_tree_meshes_as_a_box_on_a_trunk`, `an_ingested_chunk_replaces_the_guess` are new; `a_cell_keeps_...` and `a_tile_meshes_...` changed).
     - Why it looked buggy (video + screenshots): (1) a column was only its top block, so a tree was a pillar from the top leaf to the lawn with a trunk-coloured wall, and at coarse steps a 16 x 16 pillar; (2) the LoD was a second opinion of the generator: no edits, populate order not the ring's, no trees beyond 300 blocks, and it showed a guess over chunks that were already loaded (the ring draws 4 chunks, 7 stay loaded). Why it felt choppy: the index buffer was re-created for every near tile each time the eye moved 2 blocks, even when the set of covered cells had not changed.
     - Ingest: `ChunkManager::settle_around` (after a populate lands, a populate runs, or a saved chunk loads) hands a chunk over once all four populates that write into it are done (`settled`); on unload it is handed over again if it had been (edits). `take_ingest` -> `Lod::ingest` -> worker `Store::ingest` (kept in `Store::real`, laid over its tile now and in every tile made later, pruned beyond 48 chunks). The tiles it touches (and neighbours, for edge walls) are meshed again `REMESH_WAIT` = 2 s after the first chunk of a burst (Voxy's `ChunkUpdateTracker` throttle). Replaces `take_unloaded` / `far::columns`; `world/far.rs` and `lod/` stay in the tree, unused.

